t(20, 2010, """It has been reported that a number of Companions practiced and supported the juristic view adopted by Imam Abu Hanifa that a sane adult woman may marry herself off without a guardian.

Although the majority of jurists (the Malikis, Shafi'is and Hanbalis) require the guardian and hold the contract void without him, the books of jurisprudence and exegesis (especially the books of the Hanafi school, such as al-Sarakhsi's al-Mabsut) mention reports and practices of some Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, that support this view:

1. The Mother of the Believers Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her

The juristic practice: Lady Aisha is considered the most famous of those reported to have applied this view; she married her brother's daughter (Hafsa bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr) to al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, while Abd al-Rahman (the guardian) was away in the Levant. When Abd al-Rahman arrived he was angry and said: "Is this done to someone like me, and things decided without me?!" They spoke to al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, and al-Mundhir said: "The matter is in Abd al-Rahman's hands." Abd al-Rahman said: "I would not reject a matter that Aisha has decided," so the marriage was confirmed and not annulled.""")

t(21, 60, """The Hanafis took this report as evidence that Aisha conducted the marriage contract herself on behalf of her brother's daughter, without the original guardian being present at the contract session.

2. Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him

The juristic view: A report is narrated from Imam Ali ibn Abi Talib that the Hanafis cite as evidence; its gist is that he said of a woman who married off her daughter with her consent without the permission of her guardians: "The marriage is valid." He did not consider the absence of the guardian from the paternal kin (such as the uncle or brothers) to invalidate the contract, as long as the woman saw her interest in it and the other conditions were met.

3. Other Companions from whom this view was reported

Some sources and books of jurisprudence state that this view was also reported, in various ways and practices, from:

Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him.

Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both.

The Hanafi controls on the matter:

It is worth noting that Imam Abu Hanifa did not open the door for the woman to marry however she wishes without controls; rather, he made her conducting the contract herself subject to two basic conditions to protect the interest of the family and the guardian:

Suitability: that the husband be a suitable match for her (in religion, lineage, and material and social status).

The customary dowry: that she not undersell herself in the dowry and marry for less than the dowry of her peers among the women of her family.""")

